  "content": "{\n  \"paper_title\": \"Routing protocols for B2B e-commerce logistics in cyber-physical internet (CPI)\",\n  \"authors\": [\"Xinye Qu\", \"Ming Li\", \"Zhiyuan Ouyang\", \"Chung-lam Ng\", \"George Q. Huang\"],\n  \"publication_info\": \"Computers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\",\n  \"summary_by_section\": {\n    \"abstract\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The paper addresses the challenges of efficient and economical routing in B2B e-commerce logistics amidst network fragmentation. It introduces the cyber-physical internet (CPI) paradigm for logistics, which mimics how messages are sent over the internet. The authors propose a CPI framework with components like CPI routers, routing tables, and autonomous logistics systems (ALSs). They develop routing protocols for both within and among ALSs to make efficient decisions based on routing table information. The feasibility of these protocols is demonstrated through simulations of B2B e-commerce logistics scenarios.\"\n    },\n    \"introduction\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The introduction highlights how routing is crucial for B2B e-commerce logistics, which now accounts for over 90% of total e-commerce logistics. It identifies key challenges: increasing demand for timely routing, complex fragmented logistics networks, and difficulties in organizing efficient routing processes across organizations. The paper introduces the cyber-physical internet (CPI) as a solution that adds a cyber layer to the physical internet (PI) concept, enabling better collaboration between information systems and physical logistics networks. The authors present two research questions: (1) how to integrate fragmented B2B e-commerce logistics networks using CPI, and (2) how to apply datagram routing concepts from digital networks to physical package routing in CPI networks.\"\n    },\n    \"literature_review\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The literature review covers three main areas: routing problems in B2B e-commerce logistics, the physical internet (PI), and the cyber-physical internet (CPI). For B2B logistics, it discusses traditional optimization approaches including exact, heuristic, and learning-based algorithms, noting their limitations in flexibility and scalability. For PI, it traces the concept's evolution from its introduction by Montreuil (2011), highlighting how PI aims to establish open global logistics systems through standardization and interconnection. The review notes that current PI approaches still rely heavily on traditional vehicle routing problems rather than developing new paradigms for packet routing. It identifies a research gap: PI networks have not achieved information synchronization needed to integrate route and carrier information for independent decision-making.\"\n    },\n    \"cpi_networks\": {\n      \"summary\": \"This section establishes the framework for CPI networks by drawing parallels with computer networks. It explains how the data routing process in computer networks can be adapted to logistics networks, with physical packages analogous to data packets. The CPI framework adds a cyber layer to the PI, connecting logistics nodes and PI containers to routers and datagrams in cyberspace. Key components include: (1) CPI routers located at logistics nodes that handle both information and physical packages, (2) routing tables containing destination information, next hop, protocols, metrics, and transportation information, and (3) autonomous logistics systems (ALSs) that allow different logistics service providers to manage their networks independently while still connecting through routing protocols.\"\n    },\n    \"cpi_routing_protocols\": {\n      \"summary\": \"This section models the routing problem and proposes routing protocols for B2B e-commerce in CPI networks. It presents a mathematical model with objective functions minimizing cost (transit and transportation) and time. Two key routing protocols are developed: (1) ALS interior routing protocol - uses the A* algorithm to find optimal routes within an ALS through steps including router discovery, parameter information gathering, packet construction, information exchange, and route selection; (2) Routing protocol among ALSs - handles routing between different ALSs through border routers exchanging limited information about reachability and metrics, establishing neighbor relationships, and selecting optimal paths based on returned metric values.\"\n    },\n    \"numerical_study\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The authors validate their proposed CPI routing protocols through two scenarios: a small-range scenario involving three factories and two wholesalers across two ALSs, and a large-range scenario with four factories and two wholesalers across six ALSs. The simulation uses metrics like time and cost for routing decisions. Sensitivity analysis reveals that: (1) increasing the number of border routers improves network performance and reduces transportation time; (2) reducing network connectivity by decreasing CPI routers increases time and cost; and (3) using mixed metrics across ALSs generally increases both time and cost compared to using a single metric, but can occasionally yield improvements.\"\n    },\n    \"conclusions\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The paper concludes that the proposed CPI framework and routing protocols offer a viable solution to B2B e-commerce logistics routing problems. The authors highlight academic contributions in providing a CPI-driven solution to routing problems and engineering contributions in guiding the design and implementation of B2B e-commerce logistics networks. Limitations include considering only cost and time metrics, focusing on point-to-point logistics without package consolidation, and limited numerical study scale. Future research directions include incorporating consolidation and distribution scenarios, introducing time factors and periodic routing table updates, and considering competition between ALSs through pricing, time windows, and capacity constraints.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"key_contributions\": [\n    \"Introduction of a cyber-physical internet (CPI) framework that adds a cyber layer to physical internet (PI) logistics networks\",\n    \"Development of components including CPI routers and routing tables that integrate logistics nodes and routing information\",\n    \"Proposal of the autonomous logistics system (ALS) concept to manage complexity in large-scale logistics networks\",\n    \"Creation of routing protocols for both within and among ALSs to enable efficient package routing\",\n    \"Validation of the CPI framework and protocols through simulation of small and large-range B2B e-commerce logistics scenarios\"\n  ],\n  \"limitations_and_future_work\": [\n    \"Only cost and time are considered as metrics in the routing table, without constraints on logistics nodes or routes\",\n    \"B2B e-commerce scenarios are point-to-point without consolidation or distribution of physical packages\",\n    \"Limited scale of numerical study cannot prove computing times for large-scale scenarios\",\n    \"Future work should include consolidation and distribution scenarios, dynamic routing with time factors, periodic routing table updates, and competition between ALSs\"\n  ]\n}",
